Connect crystal structure, defects, and composition with material properties (@gc-crystals: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG", #general-chemistry "../solutions/Solvation-mixing-and-solubility.lingua#r_4PY91PEG2715V7F8H7MVGP4S95", #geology "../../../../nature/earth/geology/materials/Mineral-identification-and-evidence.lingua#r_4V80T5SEAWK0JPVQTJ29YSH6F4", #mineralogy-and-petrology "../../../../nature/earth/mineralogy-and-petrology/crystals/Lattices-bases-and-unit-cells.lingua#r_2HS07Q4PD6XHTVGDHBJ3SDB6J3") { r_2D2PGMBBES807ABNV8V0A46JZA
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 10’s crystalline solids and lattice exercises; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), types of solids and crystal structure; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), the structure/electronic-state connections in the later lectures.

A crystal has a useful repeating structural description, while finite size, thermal motion, defects, composition variation, and interfaces affect an actual specimen. Identify the unit cell, lattice, basis, coordination, and chemical species rather than treating a diagram of identical spheres as every solid's complete structure.

Ionic, molecular, metallic, and covalent-network models emphasize different interactions and electronic behavior. Structure and composition can influence density, mechanical response, conductivity, solubility, and chemical reactivity, but a label alone cannot predict every property. Amorphous materials and mixtures require their own structural descriptions.

For an ideal face-centered cubic monatomic cell, account for four atoms per conventional cell and use density ρ = 4M/(N_A a³) with compatible units. This relation assumes the specified occupancy and composition. Vacancies, substitution, porosity, or a different cell definition change the account.

**Work:** derive atom counts for simple cubic, body-centered, and face-centered conventional cells, then independently calculate a hypothetical density from a stated molar mass and cell length. Compare nearest-neighbor and lattice lengths geometrically. Explain a density discrepancy through possible structural or measurement changes without choosing one explanation prematurely.

Read the full solid-state chapter treatment, then connect the electronic-state Record with the actual structure. Mineralogy, materials science, crystallography, corrosion, and semiconductor growth will deepen their unique methods; this Record provides the shared bridge from a chemical formula to a structured bulk material.

**Continue:** [Explain solution formation through interactions and thermodynamic conditions](../solutions/Solvation-mixing-and-solubility.lingua#r_4PY91PEG2715V7F8H7MVGP4S95).

